## Setup
To run this project, download the comsol file from dropbox (https://www.dropbox.com/sh/b4jcgw6fe0p2937/AAB22UvhNYBiD-e7IHGHg-qpa?dl=0) and follow below steps:
* Step 1: Establish the initial conditions for transient study. 
  * Set i0=i_o_Me in ***Component-> Definitions -> Variable 1: pit kinetics***
  ![Step 1](https://github.com/anhvtnguyen/Pitting-Model/blob/main/Images/Step1.png)


  * Run Study 1 at different applied current (I_apply) using auxiliary sweep to find solution at which the criteria for pit stability is satisfied 
    C<sub>Me,bottom</sub> >= 70% C<sub>sat</sub>
  * Visualise the C<sub>Me</sub> as function of pit surface and I_apply via plot “Study 1: cMe vs x” in Results section
* Step 2: Run the transient study at the specified applied current 
  * Set i0=i_(o_Me )*f_activate2 in ***Component-> Definitions -> Variable 1: pit kinetics*** to add the sigmoid function for repassivation in the transient study for pit propagation
  * Set epsl function based on the applied current. 
   * At a low applied current when pit either repassivated completely or when new pit grows within the initial pit, there will be converging issue if epsl is too small or the change of the value is too steep at the interface. Switch to the following option: epsl with no step function or relax the step function by increasing the value of the transition zone in the step function setting  
   * At moderate to high applied current when pit can perforate the metal surface, since there is a small leak to the pseudo-phase, the step function is used to limit the values of epsl at the interface. Thus, switch to the following option: epsl with step function. 
   * Note: the parameters of the step function should be calibrated to limit the leakage issue and prevent the converging issue when epsl is too steep or too small.
  * Set the initial values for the transient study in ***Study 2->Time Dependent -> Values of dependent variables*** using the solution obtained from step 1, at which the pit stability criteria is satisfied 
   ![Step 2](https://github.com/anhvtnguyen/Pitting-Model/blob/main/Images/Step2.png)
 * Step 3: Export data to data_all.txt file.
   ![Step 3](https://github.com/anhvtnguyen/Pitting-Model/blob/main/Images/Step3.png)
 * Step 4: Process data_all.txt file using the codes in the following order: 
    1.	process_data_pit_simulation.ipynb (Jupyter notebook)
    2.	Process_Nearest_Neighbor_data_surface.ipynb (Jupyter notebook)
    3.	Phase_field_plot_singlepit.mlx (Matlab)
    4.	ExtractPlotFromComsol.mlx (Comsol Livelink with Matlab)

***Notes***
* The values of I_apply in COMSOL is calculated based on the thickness of the pseudo-third dimension. Here, it is specified to be d<sub>z</sub>=10 μm in the model for numerical converging. Assuming the thickness of 1 μm, I=I_apply/d<sub>z</sub> and will not affect the result of the model. 
* pcp_v1_run_shell_script.sh is the example script to run in the cluster.
